      "id": 1,
      "text": "Kildare sprang in the 1870s around sawmills and a Texas and Pacific Railway station, with a post office opened in 1874 named for a railroad official.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/kildare_texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 1878 Masonic Lodge Hall and First Baptist Church are now Recorded Texas Historic Landmarks, honoring Kildare’s early civic life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/kildare_texas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Kildare sits at the junction of Farm Roads 125 and 248, eight miles southeast of Linden in Cass County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/kildare-tx",
